\section{Structural Recursion}

\begin{note}
\emph{Structural recursion} is a recursive function whose recursive calls
follow the \emph{structure} of the input data.
For a linked list, the structure is ``a value and the rest of the list'',
so the recursive call is on the \lstinline{nxt} pointer of the current node.
For a binary search tree, the structure is ``a value, a left subtree, and a right subtree'',
so there are two recursive calls.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
Every structurally recursive function has the same two ingredients:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a \emph{base case} for the smallest structure, and
\item a \emph{recursive case} that combines the current value with the result of recursing on the substructure.
\end{enumerate}
For a linked list the smallest structure is a single node with \lstinline{nxt is None};
for a tree the smallest structure is the empty tree \lstinline{None}.
\end{note}
